(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-atherosclerosis-lipids-quiz"]={"title":"Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In hypertriglyceridemia, when is triglyceride-lowering therapy added?","choices":["Only when triglyceride exceeds 1000 mg/dL","Never, since triglyceride carries no risk","If still above 200 once the low-density goal is met","Before any low-density lipoprotein treatment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The primary aim is the low-density goal; if triglycerides are above 200 mg/dL after that goal is reached, additional triglyceride-lowering treatment is indicated. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"What causes the lipid elevation in familial dysbetalipoproteinemia?","choices":["Defective apolipoprotein B-100 on low-density lipoprotein","Increased clearance of high-density lipoprotein from plasma","Accumulation of chylomicron and very-low-density remnants","Reduced activity of the low-density lipoprotein receptor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An apolipoprotein E mutation, associated with apolipoprotein E2 homozygosity, lets chylomicron and very-low-density remnants accumulate, so triglyceride and total cholesterol both rise to 250 to 500 mg/dL. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What does an elevated lipoprotein(a) mean for management?","choices":["No change, since it is not a risk factor","Aggressive therapy to lower low-density lipoprotein","Reassurance, because it is a protective particle","A fibrate to bring triglyceride down first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevated lipoprotein(a) is a causal and independent risk factor for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease, so the patient needs aggressive therapy to decrease low-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Eruptive xanthomas are usually seen with extremely high levels of which lipoproteins?","choices":["Intermediate-density lipoprotein alone","Chylomicrons or very-low-density lipoprotein","Low-density lipoprotein or lipoprotein(a)","High-density lipoprotein or apolipoprotein A-I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eruptive xanthomas (clusters of small yellowish-white papules) are usually seen with extremely high chylomicrons or very-low-density lipoprotein, the triglyceride carriers. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which medication can raise triglycerides and also increase high-density lipoprotein?","choices":["Estrogen","Anabolic steroids","Glucocorticoids","Beta blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Estrogen can elevate triglyceride while increasing high-density lipoprotein; monitor patients starting birth control pills or postmenopausal estrogen, or who are pregnant. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein class carries the highest risk of atherosclerosis?","choices":["Low-density lipoprotein","Very-low-density lipoprotein","High-density lipoprotein","Chylomicrons"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein, high in cholesterol, has the highest risk; very-low-density lipoprotein (high in triglyceride) has an increased risk, and high-density lipoprotein a decreased risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the primary intervention for metabolic syndrome in all age groups?","choices":["A fibrate","Apheresis","Lifestyle therapy","A high-intensity statin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In all age groups, lifestyle therapy is the primary intervention for metabolic syndrome, and a heart-healthy lifestyle is the foundation of risk reduction across the life course. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"In very-high-risk disease with low-density lipoprotein of 70 or more on a maximal statin, where does a proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 inhibitor fit?","choices":["Only for lowering triglyceride","Only after ezetimibe is added","In place of the maximal statin","As the first add-on, before ezetimibe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If this class is considered, ezetimibe is added to the maximal statin first; the injectable is reasonable only if low-density lipoprotein stays 70 or more on both, and it is less cost-effective. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which weight-loss goal is part of therapeutic lifestyle changes?","choices":["25% of initial weight","1% of initial weight","Any weight gain is acceptable","5% to 10% of initial weight"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Therapeutic lifestyle changes call for a 5% to 10% reduction of initial weight, with moderate-intensity activity 30 minutes a day and smoking cessation. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the underlying defect in familial hypercholesterolemia?","choices":["Mutated apolipoprotein E gene","Impaired lipolysis of triglyceride","Excess clearance of high-density lipoprotein","Faulty low-density lipoprotein receptor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A genetic mutation reduces low-density lipoprotein receptor function, so low-density lipoprotein is cleared from the circulation at a reduced rate, with normal triglycerides. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Once secondary causes are excluded, why should primary causes still be sought?","choices":["They affect management of family members","They make statins unnecessary","They replace the risk estimate","They are always found on fundoscopy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary causes influence the risk of developing coronary disease, the response to drug therapy and the management of other family members, who may carry the same disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"When is coronary artery calcium scoring considered?","choices":["When clinical atherosclerotic disease is already present","When diabetes is present at age 40 to 75 years","When an intermediate-risk statin decision is uncertain","When low-density lipoprotein is 190 mg/dL or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In adults 40 to 75 without diabetes, low-density lipoprotein 70 to 189 and 10-year risk 7.5% to 19.9%, calcium scoring is considered if the statin decision is uncertain. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which apolipoprotein is made by the intestines and found in chylomicrons?","choices":["Apolipoprotein E","Apolipoprotein B-48","Apolipoprotein B-100","Apolipoprotein A-I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Apolipoprotein B-48 is synthesized by the intestines and found in chylomicrons, while B-100 is made by the liver and found in very-low-density, intermediate-density and low-density lipoprotein and lipoprotein(a). — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which is a risk-enhancing factor that favors statin therapy?","choices":["High-density lipoprotein of 60 or more","A coronary calcium score of zero","Low-density lipoprotein under 100","Persistent triglyceride of 175 or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk enhancers include persistent triglyceride of 175 mg/dL or more, family history of premature disease, persistent low-density lipoprotein of 160 or more, metabolic syndrome, chronic kidney disease and South Asian ancestry. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"How is the response to statin therapy defined?","choices":["Percentage fall in low-density lipoprotein","Absolute total cholesterol under 200","Rise in high-density lipoprotein","Resolution of xanthomas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Responses to lifestyle and statin therapy are defined by percentage reductions in low-density lipoprotein compared with baseline. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which features does familial defective apolipoprotein B-100 share with familial hypercholesterolemia?","choices":["Lipemia retinalis and recurrent acute pancreatitis","Palmar xanthomas and remnant particle accumulation","Isolated low high-density lipoprotein and obesity","Tendon xanthomas and premature atherosclerotic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Familial defective apolipoprotein B-100 causes elevated low-density lipoprotein cholesterol with tendon xanthomas and premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What determines the decision to screen for dyslipidemia?","choices":["Overall cardiovascular risk","A prior abnormal lipid panel","Visible skin xanthomas","Body weight alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The decision to screen rests on overall cardiovascular risk (age, sex, hypertension, smoking, family history of premature coronary disease, diabetes) independent of lipid levels. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which history makes a patient very high risk for future atherosclerotic events?","choices":["Multiple major atherosclerotic events","A single high-risk condition alone","Hypertension without any event","A borderline 10-year risk estimate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Very high risk means a history of multiple major events, or one major event plus multiple high-risk conditions. Major events are recent acute coronary syndrome, prior heart attack, ischemic stroke and symptomatic peripheral arterial disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A primary-prevention patient has a coronary calcium score of 150. What does it indicate?","choices":["The statin can be withheld","The score is uninterpretable","Only lifestyle is advised","Statin therapy is indicated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A score of 100 or more, or at or above the 75th percentile, indicates statin therapy unless the clinician-patient risk discussion defers it; 1 to 99 favors a statin, especially at 55 or older. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"About how much can smoking cessation raise high-density lipoprotein?","choices":["About 50%","It has no effect","Up to 10%","About 1%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation increases high-density lipoprotein up to 10%; frequent aerobic exercise adds about 5%, and losing 6 pounds raises it about 1 mg/dL. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which statement about 'normal' cholesterol levels is accurate?","choices":["Untreated levels do not predict disease","There are no normal levels for cholesterol","Normal levels are fixed across every population","Very low cholesterol causes known side effects"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. There are no 'normal' cholesterol levels, and no evidence that adult cholesterol can be too low, but untreated values predict higher cardiovascular morbidity and mortality. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the typical low-density lipoprotein range in heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia?","choices":["100 to 129 mg/dL","200 to 400 mg/dL","130 to 159 mg/dL","Above 1000 mg/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heterozygous form is characterized by low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 mg/dL; the homozygous form runs total cholesterol from about 400 to above 1000 mg/dL. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"In adults aged 20 to 39, when should a statin be considered?","choices":["Family history of premature disease and low-density of 160 or more","Any low-density lipoprotein above 100 mg/dL without other risk","Only after a calcium score above 1000","Never, since statins are reserved for those over 40"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At 20 to 39 lifetime risk is estimated to encourage lifestyle, and a statin is considered with a family history of premature disease and low-density lipoprotein of 160 or more. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which drug class is the most effective for lowering low-density lipoprotein?","choices":["Fibrates","Niacin","Ezetimibe","Statins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Statins are the most effective medication for lowering low-density lipoprotein, by 25% to 63% depending on intensity, with maximum results in 4 to 6 weeks; they also raise high-density lipoprotein 5% to 22%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is the most devastating complication of homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia?","choices":["Loss of vision","Accelerated atherosclerosis","Recurrent acute pancreatitis","Hepatosplenomegaly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The most devastating complication is accelerated atherosclerosis, which can result in disability and sudden death in childhood, after symptomatic atherosclerosis before puberty. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which lipid pattern is typical of severe hypertriglyceridemia?","choices":["High triglyceride, low high-density, normal low-density","High low-density, normal triglyceride, high total","High triglyceride and total both near 250 to 500","High triglyceride over 150 and low-density over 130"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 500 mg/dL with elevated total cholesterol and reduced high-density lipoprotein, usually without elevated low-density lipoprotein or apolipoprotein B. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Where do the eruptive xanthomas of familial chylomicronemia syndrome cluster?","choices":["Palms and palmar creases","Eyelids near the inner canthus","Back, buttocks and extensor limbs","Achilles and patellar tendons"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are clusters of small yellowish-white papules on the back, buttocks and extensor surfaces of the arms and legs, alongside lipemia retinalis and pancreatitis. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which of these is a statin?","choices":["Gemfibrozil","Alirocumab","Pitavastatin","Colesevelam"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The statins are atorvastatin, fluvastatin, lovastatin, pravastatin, rosuvastatin, simvastatin and pitavastatin, all named by their generic '-statin' ending. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of dyslipidemia in children?","choices":["High-carbohydrate diet","Hypothyroidism","Medications","Primary genetic types"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The primary (genetic) types are the most common etiology of dyslipidemia in children, arising from overproduction or defective clearance of triglyceride or low-density lipoprotein, or loss of high-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the leading cause of death in men and women in the United States?","choices":["Peripheral arterial disease","Coronary heart disease","Ischemic stroke","Acute pancreatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coronary heart disease is the leading cause of death in men and women in the United States, which is why diagnosing and treating dyslipidemia matters for lowering that risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
